---
// Privacy policy for the Lister desktop app. This is the URL entered as the
// "Privacy Policy URL" against both eBay keysets (Sandbox + Production) when
// configuring the RuName, so it has to describe the app accurately: local-first
// processing, encrypted local token storage, and a static callback page that
// collects nothing. The website itself is covered at /privacy/.
import PolicyLayout from '../../layouts/PolicyLayout.astro';
---

<PolicyLayout
  title="Lister — Privacy Policy"
  description="How the Lister desktop app handles your data: processed locally, sent only to eBay, with credentials stored encrypted on your own computer."
  scope="How the Lister desktop app and its eBay sign-in page handle data."
>
  <p>
    <strong>Lister</strong> is a desktop application from LittleLordGames that helps
    a seller build eBay listings from a folder of photos. It runs on your own computer.
    This policy explains what it does with your data, and what the eBay sign-in page
    hosted on littlelordgames.com does.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Your photos and listing details are processed <strong>locally</strong>, on your machine.</li>
    <li>
      The only service they are sent to is <strong>eBay</strong>, and only to create
      or update your own listings.
    </li>
    <li>
      Your eBay credentials and sign-in tokens are stored <strong>encrypted on your
      computer</strong>, using your operating system's secure store. They are never
      sent to littlelordgames.com.
    </li>
    <li>
      The sign-in page on this site is a <strong>static page</strong>. It does not
      collect, store, log, or transmit your authorization code — there is no server
      behind it to receive one.
    </li>
    <li>Nothing is sold or shared with third parties.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What Lister processes on your computer</h2>
  <p>
    Lister reads the folder of images you point it at and the listing details you
    type in — titles, descriptions, prices, quantities, and part names. Images are
    resized and prepared locally, in a working folder on your own machine. All of
    this stays on your computer unless and until you choose to publish a listing.
  </p>

  <h2>What is sent to eBay</h2>
  <p>When you publish, Lister sends the following to eBay on your behalf:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Your prepared images</strong>, uploaded to eBay's own picture hosting
      so the listing can reference them. Images are not hosted by LittleLordGames or
      by anyone else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your listing details</strong> — title, description, prices,
      quantities, and variations — submitted to eBay's selling APIs under your own
      eBay account.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Once data reaches eBay it is governed by eBay's own terms, including its
    <a
      href="https://www.ebay.co.uk/help/policies/member-behaviour-policies/user-privacy-notice-privacy-policy?id=4260"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener noreferrer">User Privacy Notice</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>Your eBay credentials and tokens</h2>
  <p>
    To use Lister you supply your own eBay developer credentials and sign in to
    your eBay account. Your application credentials, your redirect name (RuName),
    and the OAuth access and refresh tokens issued by eBay are stored
    <strong>only on your own computer</strong>, encrypted through your operating
    system's secure store — Keychain on macOS, DPAPI on Windows, or libsecret on
    Linux.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your operating system cannot provide that encryption, Lister keeps these
    values in memory for that session only and tells you so, rather than writing
    them to disk unprotected.
  </p>
  <p>
    These credentials and tokens are never transmitted to littlelordgames.com, to
    LittleLordGames, or to any third party. They go only to eBay, as part of
    signing in and making requests on your behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>The eBay sign-in page on this site</h2>
  <p>
    eBay requires the page it returns you to after sign-in to be served over
    HTTPS, which a desktop application cannot provide by itself. So
    littlelordgames.com hosts a single static page that acts as the bridge back to
    the app running on your computer.
  </p>
  <p>That page:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      reads the short-lived authorization code that eBay puts in the address bar,
      and forwards your browser to the copy of Lister listening on your own machine
      (<code>127.0.0.1</code>) so it can complete sign-in;
    </li>
    <li>
      shows you the same code with a copy button, in case you need to paste it into
      the app by hand;
    </li>
    <li>
      removes the code from the address bar and from your browser history once it
      has done so.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It is a single self-contained HTML file. It loads no scripts, fonts, styles,
    images, or analytics from anywhere, sets no cookies, and makes no requests of
    its own. Your authorization code is never sent to a LittleLordGames server —
    the page is static, so there is no server-side component that could receive,
    log, or store it. The code is exchanged for tokens by the app on your computer,
    talking directly to eBay.
  </p>

  <h2>Analytics and cookies</h2>
  <p>
    The Lister application contains no analytics or telemetry, and does not report
    your usage anywhere. The eBay sign-in page loads no analytics and sets no
    cookies.
  </p>
  <p>
    This page you are reading now is part of the littlelordgames.com website,
    which counts page views using a cookieless analytics service. That is described
    in the <a href="/privacy/">website privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Third parties</h2>
  <p>
    Your data is not sold, rented, or shared. Apart from eBay — the service you are
    deliberately listing on — no third party receives your images, listing details,
    credentials, or tokens.
  </p>

  <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
  <p>
    Because everything lives on your own computer, you remove it by signing out in
    Lister (which discards the stored tokens), deleting the app's data folder, or
    uninstalling the app. You can also revoke Lister's access at any time from your
    eBay account settings.
  </p>
</PolicyLayout>
